To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function. The `exit()` function is a part of the `stdlib.h` library in C. When you call `exit()`, it sends a signal to the operating system to terminate the program.

Here's an example of how to use `exit()` in a C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");

    // Terminate the program
    exit(0);

    // This line will not be executed
    printf("This will not be printed.\n");

    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, after printing "Hello, World!", the `exit(0)` statement is called to terminate the program. The number 0 is a common convention to indicate successful termination.

If you want to terminate the program from within a function, you can use the `return` statement with an integer value. The value returned to the calling function can be used to indicate the success or failure of the program.

Here's an example of terminating a program from within a function:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void myFunction() {
    printf("Inside myFunction.\n");

    // Terminate the program
    return;
}

int main() {
    printf("Before calling myFunction.\n");

    myFunction();

    printf("After calling myFunction.\n");

    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, the `myFunction()` terminates when the `return` statement is encountered, causing the program to exit. The `main()` function will not execute the line after the function call.

Remember to include the `stdlib.h` library in your program to use the `exit()` function.
